human_labels.body:
Since there is only two strings, it would be simpler to order them with an `if`. The latter should be more efficient, unless Go manages to optimize the `sort.Strings()` to something similar. Though even then, I think it is beneficial to have simple code which is clearly efficient, rather than more complex code which might be efficient if compiler does its job well.

```suggestion
			dupl1, dupl2 := rs.Metric.String(), duplSample.Metric.String()
			if dupl1 > dupl2 {
				// Make the order deterministic.
				dupl1, dupl2 = dupl2, dupl1
			}
```

Done. Replaced the slice + `sort.Strings` with a compare-and-swap:

```go
dupl1, dupl2 := rs.Metric.String(), duplSample.Metric.String()
if dupl1 > dupl2 {
	// Make the order deterministic.
	dupl1, dupl2 = dupl2, dupl1
}
```

The `errorf` call now passes `dupl1, dupl2`, and I kept the existing `// Many-to-many matching not allowed.` comment.

Done — trimmed to two lines:

```go
// The expected pair is sorted lexicographically; Labels.String() renders
// __name__ as a regular label, so it sorts before "label" in each set.
```

While here I also corrected the function doc comment: it had said the two strings came "directly from rightSigs map iteration order", but the collision branch indexes `rightSigs[sigOrd]` (a `[]Sample` slice) by ordinal rather than ranging a map. The actual source of variation is the upstream right-hand-side arrival order (which can derive from Go map iteration when the RHS is an aggregation), so the comment now reflects that.

